WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.307
Rust offers signed and unsigned integers
in several widths,

2
00:00:03.387 --> 00:00:05.313
plus pointer-sized `usize` and `isize`.

3
00:00:05.393 --> 00:00:07.539
The right type depends on what the value
means.

4
00:00:07.619 --> 00:00:10.811
A count, length, array index, protocol
field, database identifier,

5
00:00:10.891 --> 00:00:13.533
user-visible value, and signed delta may
all be numbers,

6
00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:16.145
but they do not all carry the same design
requirements.

7
00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:19.362
Signed types communicate that values below
zero may be meaningful.

8
00:00:19.442 --> 00:00:22.029
That can fit deltas, balances, offsets,
temperatures,

9
00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:24.585
or calculations where negative values are
expected.

10
00:00:24.665 --> 00:00:27.252
Unsigned types communicate non-negative
quantities,

11
00:00:27.332 --> 00:00:29.533
but unsigned does not automatically mean
safe.

12
00:00:29.613 --> 00:00:32.750
A value can be non-negative and still be
too large, in the wrong unit,

13
00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.206
or invalid for the operation.

14
00:00:34.286 --> 00:00:37.478
Fixed-width types are useful when layout,
serialization, storage,

15
00:00:37.558 --> 00:00:39.264
or protocol compatibility matters.

16
00:00:39.344 --> 00:00:41.326
A file format might define a field as
`u16`,

17
00:00:41.406 --> 00:00:43.167
a network message might carry a `u32`,

18
00:00:43.247 --> 00:00:45.723
and an embedded interface might require
exact width.

19
00:00:45.803 --> 00:00:48.830
In those cases, the type choice documents
the external contract.

20
00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:52.432
`usize` is appropriate for indexing and
memory sizes because it matches the

21
00:00:52.512 --> 00:00:54.329
platform's addressable memory model.

22
00:00:54.409 --> 00:00:56.885
But it should not become the default for
every number.

23
00:00:56.965 --> 00:00:59.112
A port, retry count, percentage, database
ID,

24
00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:02.549
or protocol version may deserve a
domain-specific type or a fixed-width

25
00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:03.840
representation instead.

26
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.671
Defensive Rust chooses integer types that
express intent.

27
00:01:06.751 --> 00:01:07.742
If a type is too broad,

28
00:01:07.822 --> 00:01:11.564
review becomes harder because invalid
values can flow farther into the program.

29
00:01:11.644 --> 00:01:15.057
If a type is too narrow, conversion and
overflow edges become more likely.

30
00:01:15.137 --> 00:01:18.439
The best choice makes valid range,
signedness, platform assumptions,

31
00:01:18.519 --> 00:01:20.500
and boundary behavior easy to understand.
